{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Akh</b> — The reason for <b>akh</b> [is]: why does it say 'my soul is silent' [נפשי דומיה]? Like the sense of 'Be silent to her' [דום לה], 'Be silent until we reach you' [דומו עד הגיענו אליכם], on the pattern of 'Over these I weep' [על אלה אני בוכיה] — that salvation comes to her from the Name alone."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 62, :notes {0 "The comment quotes six Hebrew phrases (from Psa 62:2, Hab 2:20, 1Sam 10:5 [?], Lam 1:16 [?], and a phrase about salvation to 'her' [Jerusalem/Israel]) to argue that the silences in each share a single grammatical pattern; all are rendered in Hebrew script with minimal glosses."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:32:30.870629470Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}